d that  to the expression no problem and that  really gives you that deep level  information of okay well what's  happening in production right now so if  you've ever trouble shot an issue that  you could not reproduce in in product or  sorry you couldn't reproduce in QA  debuggers for you if you've ever  forgotten to add a line of log statement  that you wish you had debuggers for you  so these are the kind of problems we try  to solve and with that being said I'm  gonna hand things over to my colleague  Morgan to talk about profiler thanks  Bryan  so if you attended cloud next last year  you probably would have seen that we  announced act rubber profilers public  beta on stage the keynote and during the  APM breakout since then we've had a ton  of customer success stories of profiler  I'll get into those in a minute but  we're really really proud of how far the  product has come for those who aren't  already aware of profiler stackdriver  profiler is a production code  performance analysis tool  it runs continually on your production  applications with effectively no  performance impact and allows you to  dive into code all the way down to the  function level to see where your code is  being slowed and where it's costing you  resources profiler today works on  applications that use Java node go and  Python we recently added Python support  and as of today profiler is now  generally available which is what we're  really excited that now talk is cheap  and it's difficult to describe some of  these products from from a high-level so  I'm gonna dive into a demo to show you  how we can use profiler to debug  performance problems on a running  production application perfect so this  is one of the instance of the hipster  shop demo that you've probably seen at  many many google talks it's a micro  services application thinks the same  when the Brian was using in his demo  with a whole bunch of services inside of  it written in different languages right  now we're using profiler to inspect the  product catalog service the service I  think is written in go and it's the  database of products inside this fake  e-commerce application so to start  profiler aggregates your performance  data down by the service this product  catalog service I think has about five  instances we reviewing the performance  information across all of those as I  mentioned this is running in production  with effectively no performance impact  which is what separates stackdriver  profiler from most other profiling tools  it runs continually you don't turn it on  for a brief period and then turn it off  again you don't have to turn it on and  freak out that it's going to take down  your system by adding extra load it's  very efficient it's very intuitive  profiler can inspect your the  performance of your code across CPU  performance memory performance and  thread contention today we're gonna look  at CPU you can of course break down your  profiles across zones or different  versions of your app we're not going to  do that today but it's available and  there's some other filters that get to  in a minute by default the  is what you see this is a flame graph if  you've used other profiling tools you're  probably familiar if this on the flame  graph on the horizontal axis we're  showing the performance cost of every  function each box that you see on the  flame graph represents a function in  your code base and so functions that are  taking more horizontal space so like the  one I'm messing over take up more  basically costing you more in terms of  performance and money on the vertical  axis we're showing you the call tree so  in this case we've got search stream  stuff func one dot one that calls handle  stream that calls process unary RPC and  so on and so right away we can tell that  we're burning about half our CPU on this  big stack on the left and if we dive  into it we'll discover that this is  related to JSON serialization and  deserialization probably not something  you want to be spending a lot of time on  on a catalog service that's effectively  a database we'll come back to the in  depth performance of that in a minute  so flame graphs are great for finding  out problems where you have like one big  function in your code base and one big  stack that's consuming a lot of  resources and yeah we've had a lot of  customers who've had got just like these  but profiler is a lot more powerful and  just finding that what I'm gonna open  next is what we call the focus table the  focus table shows us where we're burning  CPU aggregated across all functions you  can sort this by the self consumption  self consumption of a function is the  amount of CPU or memory burned by that  function but not by its children or you  can soda by it sorted by the total  consumption in this case we're gonna  zoom in here on this on the state and  string call which is burning over 5% of  our CPU this brings us to the focus view  so profiler isn't just useful for  finding the performance impact of your  code it's also useful for finding how  your code actually calls itself in  production this focus view has taken  every instance of State and string in  our application and showing us the  different ways that we can get there  from route and how the performance the  performance cost of each of those call  paths this particular case that's not  telling us anything really interesting  what is interesting about focus is that  it'll also show you all the ways out of  that function and by we can observe that  there are none so there's there's no  other functions listed under state and  string that tells us that if we're task  to perform improving the performance of  the service statement string is a good  place to start  because it's burning its own CPU it's  bringing over 5% of the CPU aggregated  across the entire system and finally  it's not it's not spending that time in  its child functions if you want to  optimize it you can optimize that  functio